            <ArticleTitle>College students' perceived disease risk versus actual prevalence rates.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>96-106</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare college students' perceived disease risk with disease prevalence rates.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data were analyzed from 625 college students collected with an Internet-based survey. Paired t-tests were used to separately compare participants' perceived 10-year and lifetime disease risk for 4 diseases: heart disease, cancer, diabetes, and overweight/obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Respondents estimated their risk of developing heart disease as lower than cancer, yet rated their risk of developing heart disease as higher than diabetes and being overweight/obese.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Incongruence between college students' perceived disease risk and disease prevalence rates calls for improved public health education.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
